26
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,470
So we're going to
start with this today.

27
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:00,387
The question is, in
Markov decision processes,

28
00:01:00,387 --> 00:01:02,208
a large discount
factor gamma means

29
00:01:02,208 --> 00:01:04,250
that short-term rewards
are much more influential

30
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:06,060
than long-term rewards?

31
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:09,380
And then a second question
to start thinking about is,

32
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:10,440
in general--

33
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:13,220
so last time we started talking
about sequential decision making

34
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:15,650
under uncertainty, and
one of the things we often

35
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:19,380
would like in real-world systems
is monotonic improvement,

36
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:22,890
meaning that if we get more
data or we get more computation,

37
00:01:22,890 --> 00:01:25,830
we know that the system is going
to be better, make, in our case,

38
00:01:25,830 --> 00:01:28,040
better decisions than
it could if it had

39
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,350
less computation or less data.

40
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:33,488
And so the question that
I'm posing to you now

41
00:01:33,488 --> 00:01:35,030
and that we're going
to discuss today

42
00:01:35,030 --> 00:01:37,460
is, is it possible to
construct algorithms

43
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,700
for computing decision policies
so that we can guarantee

44
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,620
with additional computation--

45
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:45,170
we can also think of
often as iteration--

46
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:47,090
that we're going to
monotonically improve

47
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:48,577
the decision policy?

48
00:01:48,577 --> 00:01:50,660
And you can start to think
about if you're already

49
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:53,270
aware of any algorithms that
might have that property, if you

50
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:56,250
think it's impossible, or
if you think-- if it's true,

51
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:59,650
do you think that all
algorithms would satisfy that?

52
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:00,650
That's not for the poll.

53
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:02,390
That's just to start
thinking about,

54
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:05,600
and we'll come back to it later.

55
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,100
All right.

56
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:07,540
So I'll just give you
another minute or two

57
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:09,123
to do this Refresh
Your Understanding.

58
00:02:09,123 --> 00:02:10,960
It's just a quick one,
and then we'll go.

59
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:26,930
And again, these are not
assessment questions,

60
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:28,930
so you're welcome to look
back on lecture slides

61
00:02:28,930 --> 00:02:29,800
from last time.

62
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:32,175
You're also welcome to talk
to anybody right next to you.

63
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:08,610
All right.

64
00:03:08,610 --> 00:03:13,250
It looks like we actually have
maybe a 2/3-1/3 split on this

65
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:14,330
question.

66
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:16,632
The correct answer is false.

67
00:03:16,632 --> 00:03:18,340
Does somebody want to
say why it's false?

68
00:03:21,190 --> 00:03:21,690
Yeah?

69
00:03:21,690 --> 00:03:23,710
And remind me your name.

70
00:03:23,710 --> 00:03:26,770
Yeah, I think because you
multiply the longer-term rewards

71
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:28,162
by the gamma.

72
00:03:28,162 --> 00:03:30,370
So a large gamma means that
the long-term rewards are

73
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:32,350
weighted decently [INAUDIBLE].

74
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:32,930
That's right.

75
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:34,420
So if you have-- exactly
what [AUDIO OUT] said.

76
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:35,830
So if gamma was
1, you would care

77
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:39,080
about short-term rewards exactly
the same as long-term rewards.

78
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:41,200
In general, if gamma
was 0, you would not

79
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:42,730
care about long
term rewards at all.

80
00:03:42,730 --> 00:03:44,200
You'd be entirely myopic.

81
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,480
But as gamma gets
closer to 1, it's

82
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:49,000
sort of a relatively weighting
more of longer rewards

83
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,270
than you would otherwise.

84
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,230
Great.

85
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:55,730
All right.

86
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:57,490
And yes, as I said,
we'll get more

87
00:03:57,490 --> 00:03:59,800
into the conceptual
question later.

88
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,550
The other thing that
I wanted to clarify--

89
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:03,670
I saw there was some
questions on this last time

90
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:05,770
as well as after class
as well as on Ed--

91
00:04:05,770 --> 00:04:09,520
is I had mentioned when I
was making distinguishments

92
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:12,430
between reinforcement learning
and other forms of AI machine

93
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:14,960
learning this notion
of optimization.

94
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:17,677
But I think that that
was a little bit--

95
00:04:17,677 --> 00:04:19,760
I think it was more confusing
than it was helpful,

96
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,840
and depending on
how you think of it,

97
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:24,520
in machine learning
or AI, we always

98
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:26,810
have some form of
metric or optimization.

99
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:28,870
So you can think
of a loss as also

100
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:30,650
being-- we're trying
to minimize the loss,

101
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:33,350
and so that also sounds like
an optimization problem.

102
00:04:33,350 --> 00:04:35,660
So you can just ignore
that distinction for now.

103
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:37,240
I do think in
general, when we're

104
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:39,670
thinking about
decision-making, it's

105
00:04:39,670 --> 00:04:42,320
going to be very important what
we think of as that metric.

106
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:44,660
And so it won't necessarily
just be loss functions.

107
00:04:44,660 --> 00:04:47,110
We can have lots of different
scalar values or even

108
00:04:47,110 --> 00:04:48,530
multiple objectives.

109
00:04:48,530 --> 00:04:51,190
But the distinction of whether
or not supervised learning

110
00:04:51,190 --> 00:04:54,360
is using optimization is
perhaps not so helpful.

111
00:04:57,080 --> 00:04:57,690
OK, great.

112
00:04:57,690 --> 00:04:59,660
So let's go ahead
and get started.

113
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:03,780
So I do also just want to
highlight that for some of you--

114
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:05,370
and I got a question about this.

115
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:07,370
We've also got a couple
of questions about this.

116
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:09,500
This first week or two
will overlap a little bit

117
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,667
with some of the other
classes you might have taken.

118
00:05:11,667 --> 00:05:14,640
So particularly if you've taken
238 with Mykel Kochenderfer,

119
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,082
the beginning may overlap.

120
00:05:16,082 --> 00:05:17,540
The things that
will probably still

121
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:19,248
be different in the
first couple of weeks

122
00:05:19,248 --> 00:05:22,670
is I expect there's going to
be a higher level of theory

123
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:25,610
in the first week or two
about the properties of some

124
00:05:25,610 --> 00:05:28,700
of these algorithms and what
sort of guarantees we have.

125
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,737
And then afterwards,
I suspect after that,

126
00:05:30,737 --> 00:05:32,570
most of the content in
the rest of the class

127
00:05:32,570 --> 00:05:34,008
will be quite different.

128
00:05:34,008 --> 00:05:36,050
If you have any questions
about how this compares

129
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:38,120
to a lot of the other
decision-making classes

130
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:39,650
that are offered
at Stanford, don't

131
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:42,140
hesitate to reach out to
me in office hours on Ed

132
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:44,390
or after class.

133
00:05:44,390 --> 00:05:45,120
All right.

134
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:46,280
Now, why do we do this?

135
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:47,780
Because also, you
might be thinking,

136
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:49,280
we want to get to
AlphaGo, or I want

137
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:50,720
to get to controlling
robots, or I

138
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:52,380
want to get to optimizing LLMs.

139
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:56,000
Why are we starting with systems
like the seven-state Mars rover

140
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,480
that we're going to look at.

141
00:05:57,480 --> 00:05:59,610
And the reason is
because actually a lot

142
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:04,860
of the ideas that enabled people
to solve AlphaGo and do things

143
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:07,770
like RLHF, or reinforcement
learning from human feedback,

144
00:06:07,770 --> 00:06:10,800
really builds on this
fundamental notion of decision

145
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:11,800
processes.

146
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,250
And I think it's much
easier to really cleanly see

147
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:17,130
how these ideas come up
when you can actually see

148
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:19,090
these in the world as tabular.

149
00:06:19,090 --> 00:06:20,890
You can just write
down all the states.

150
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:22,630
So that's why I
think it's helpful,

151
00:06:22,630 --> 00:06:24,660
but even today we're going
to start to see where

152
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:26,097
those ideas might be applied.

153
00:06:26,097 --> 00:06:28,680
So we're going to start to do
things like policy search, which

154
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:30,240
is the foundations
towards things

155
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:33,207
like policy gradients, which
are extremely widely used.

156
00:06:33,207 --> 00:06:35,790
So you can think of all of these
as just being building blocks

157
00:06:35,790 --> 00:06:37,985
that we're going to use to build
up to get to the point where

158
00:06:37,985 --> 00:06:39,610
we're later going to
be-- and very soon,

159
00:06:39,610 --> 00:06:40,693
within a couple of weeks--

160
00:06:40,693 --> 00:06:43,860
tackling things that are
state-of-the-art algorithms.

161
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:44,380
All right.

162
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:45,360
So what we're going
to be doing today

163
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:48,180
is really focusing on making
good decisions given a Markov

164
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:50,460
decision process,
and so that means

165
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:53,100
both being able to understand
how good a particular decision

166
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:58,610
policy is as well as what is
an optimal decision policy.

167
00:06:58,610 --> 00:07:02,480
And when I say we're given a
model of the world, what I mean

168
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:06,590
is that we are given that
dynamics model, which tells us

169
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:12,350
how the world evolves
when we make decisions,

170
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:14,930
and we are given a reward
model, which tells us

171
00:07:14,930 --> 00:07:17,660
how good decisions are.

172
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:20,790
And last time we talked
about Markov processes,

173
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:23,480
and we were starting to talk
about Markov reward processes

174
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:26,540
because they can end up being
really useful when we're

175
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:30,610
trying to evaluate how good a
particular decision policy is.

176
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:32,240
And we'll see a lot
of the same ideas

177
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,060
from Markov reward
processes to MDPs.

178
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:37,560
All right.

179
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:38,935
So let's just
refresh our memory.

180
00:07:38,935 --> 00:07:41,600
So this is the question
that we had before of,

181
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:45,690
how do we think of the
influence of discount factors?

182
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:48,320
As was said, what happens is
we multiply the next reward

183
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:51,950
by the discount factor two
rewards away by the discount

184
00:07:51,950 --> 00:07:53,930
factor squared, et cetera.

185
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:57,020
And so as you can see there,
if the horizon is really long

186
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:58,940
or as it goes to
infinity, rewards

187
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:03,470
will have 0 value eventually
because gamma is less than 1.

188
00:08:03,470 --> 00:08:06,080
So the idea of the value
function was to say--

189
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,830
remember, this is a
Markov reward process.

190
00:08:07,830 --> 00:08:08,997
We don't have decisions yet.

191
00:08:08,997 --> 00:08:12,440
It just says, how much is
the expected discounted

192
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:14,930
sum of rewards we will
get starting in this state

193
00:08:14,930 --> 00:08:17,970
and acting, most of the
time today, forever?

194
00:08:17,970 --> 00:08:19,707
So most of the time
we'll think of today

195
00:08:19,707 --> 00:08:22,040
of just getting to act forever
and how much reward would

196
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:22,920
you get.

197
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:25,640
And because this gamma-- as
long as the gamma is less than 1

198
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:29,585
here, that will be
a finite number.

199
00:08:29,585 --> 00:08:32,210
So we're just starting to talk
about how could we compute this.

200
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:34,600
So again, remember,
the return is

201
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,850
going to be a particular
series of rewards

202
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:39,400
you might get if you start in
this state and act forever,

203
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:41,267
and V is going to
be, on average,

204
00:08:41,267 --> 00:08:43,600
how much reward would you get
if you start in this state

205
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:46,730
and act forever?

206
00:08:46,730 --> 00:08:47,340
All right.

207
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:49,760
So one of the key ideas
here is that computing

208
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:54,080
the value of an infinite
horizon Markov reward process

209
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:56,300
leverages the Markov
property, which

210
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,840
was this idea that the future
is independent of the past given

211
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:00,627
the present.

212
00:09:00,627 --> 00:09:02,210
So given your current
state, you don't

213
00:09:02,210 --> 00:09:04,670
have to think more
about the history.

214
00:09:04,670 --> 00:09:07,070
So what that implies
when we try to compute

215
00:09:07,070 --> 00:09:09,963
what the expected reward is,
future reward is from a state is

216
00:09:09,963 --> 00:09:12,380
we can think of, well, what
is the immediate reward we get

217
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:15,080
in that state plus all
the different states

218
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:17,510
we could get to next
under our dynamics model

219
00:09:17,510 --> 00:09:20,890
and then the value
of their reward?

220
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:22,400
How much do we
weigh each of those?

221
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:23,470
Well, we weigh
each of those just

222
00:09:23,470 --> 00:09:25,470
according to what is the
probability I could get

223
00:09:25,470 --> 00:09:28,283
to each of those next states.

224
00:09:28,283 --> 00:09:30,450
And if you're familiar with
things like tree search,

225
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:32,370
you can think of it as just,
I'm in my starting state.

226
00:09:32,370 --> 00:09:34,230
I think of all the next
states I could go to.

227
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:36,688
Each of them have some weight,
depending on the probability

228
00:09:36,688 --> 00:09:38,960
I get there, and then
I sum all of those

229
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:40,180
up according to their values.

230
00:09:43,305 --> 00:09:44,680
And this is going
to be the basis

231
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:48,880
of the Bellman equation, which
we're going to see lots about.

232
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:52,460
So if we wanted to think
about how we could solve this,

233
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:55,840
one way we could think of it
as if we have a tabular world,

234
00:09:55,840 --> 00:10:01,060
meaning that we can maintain a
scalar value for every single

235
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:04,180
state separately-- so this
is like our Mars rover case--

236
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:06,310
then we could just
express the value function

237
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:07,870
in a matrix equation.

238
00:10:07,870 --> 00:10:09,670
So we say the value
of each of the states

239
00:10:09,670 --> 00:10:11,650
is exactly equal to
the immediate reward

240
00:10:11,650 --> 00:10:15,520
plus gamma times the
transition probability

241
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:18,740
to all the next states.

242
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:21,470
And so that's nice because
now we can just directly

243
00:10:21,470 --> 00:10:23,370
solve for what the value is.

244
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:26,063
So we know that
this has to halt,

245
00:10:26,063 --> 00:10:28,480
so now what we're going to do
is just invert that to solve

246
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,090
for V. So what we
would say in this case

247
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:38,060
is we would say v minus gamma
times P of V-- this is P.

248
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:42,093
And again, I'll apologize
that in the different things

249
00:10:42,093 --> 00:10:44,510
you see online or the textbook,
et cetera people sometimes

250
00:10:44,510 --> 00:10:46,440
use T for transition matrix.

251
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:48,650
They sometimes use
P for probabilities,

252
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:49,940
going to the next state.

253
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:52,170
If it's ever confusing what
notation is being used,

254
00:10:52,170 --> 00:10:54,080
don't hesitate to reach out.

255
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:58,260
OK, so we just rewrite it like
this, as equal to R, and then

256
00:10:58,260 --> 00:10:58,990
we move this.

257
00:10:58,990 --> 00:11:02,910
So we have V of I
minus gamma P is

258
00:11:02,910 --> 00:11:10,880
equal to RI equals the
identity matrix, which

259
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:19,560
means V is equal to I minus
gamma P inverse times R.

260
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:20,830
So why do I show this?

261
00:11:20,830 --> 00:11:23,050
I show this because if you
know how the world works,

262
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:25,258
you have the dynamics model,
you know what the reward

263
00:11:25,258 --> 00:11:27,490
function is, and the
world is small enough,

264
00:11:27,490 --> 00:11:29,970
you can just directly
solve for this.

265
00:11:29,970 --> 00:11:31,230
This isn't for decision yet.

266
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:34,800
This is just showing
us what the value

267
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:36,630
would be of each of the states.

268
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:38,410
So this is one way to solve it.

269
00:11:38,410 --> 00:11:42,360
We would call this
the analytic solution.

270
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:46,500
And one thing to note here is
this requires a matrix inverse,

271
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:48,930
and so there are faster
algorithms than N cubed,

272
00:11:48,930 --> 00:11:50,170
N being the number of states.

273
00:11:50,170 --> 00:11:53,400
But in general, matrix
inverses are fairly expensive.

274
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:56,910
So this is being done once,
but this is a fairly--

275
00:11:56,910 --> 00:11:59,830
if your state space, the number
of states you have N, is large,

276
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:01,120
this can be expensive.

277
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:04,500
And it also requires
that the identity matrix

278
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:08,310
minus gamma times the
dynamics, the dynamics model

279
00:12:08,310 --> 00:12:09,828
is invertible.

280
00:12:09,828 --> 00:12:11,620
OK, so this is one way
we could solve this.

281
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:12,120
Yeah?

282
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:14,370
And remind me your name.

283
00:12:14,370 --> 00:12:17,380
In practice, what
usually happens?

284
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:21,570
Do people just go ahead and
take the matrix inverse?

285
00:12:21,570 --> 00:12:22,710
Let me reword the question.

286
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:25,980
In practice, do you usually find
that these kinds of matrices

287
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:27,360
are invertible?

288
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:29,940
And if yes, do people just
go ahead and take the matrix

289
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:31,835
inverse, or do they
[INAUDIBLE] something?

290
00:12:31,835 --> 00:12:32,710
It's a good question.

291
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:34,200
So in practice,
is it invertible,

292
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:35,130
and what do people do?

293
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:36,630
In practice, normally,
we're dealing

294
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:39,600
with state spaces that are far
too large, so we can't do this.

295
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:42,090
Yeah, good question.

296
00:12:42,090 --> 00:12:44,890
There might be cases where it's
small enough, but in general,

297
00:12:44,890 --> 00:12:45,600
no.

298
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:48,240
So that's a great motivation
for a second approach, which

299
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:50,320
is instead of doing it
directly analytically,

300
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:51,945
we're going to use
dynamic programming,

301
00:12:51,945 --> 00:12:54,147
and we're going to design
an iterative algorithm.

302
00:12:54,147 --> 00:12:56,730
And this is going to be very,
very similar to what we're going

303
00:12:56,730 --> 00:12:58,510
to see for decision processes.

304
00:12:58,510 --> 00:13:00,090
So the idea in this
case is we're not

305
00:13:00,090 --> 00:13:01,320
going to do this in
one step, but we're

306
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:03,180
going to avoid that
matrix inverse, which

307
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:04,690
might be pretty expensive.

308
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:08,820
So we're going to initialize the
value of a state to 0 for all s,

309
00:13:08,820 --> 00:13:11,050
and you can think about
whether or not it actually

310
00:13:11,050 --> 00:13:12,710
matters what we initialize to.

311
00:13:12,710 --> 00:13:14,260
But just imagine we do that.

312
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:16,310
And then for a
series of iterations,

313
00:13:16,310 --> 00:13:17,620
k is our iteration variable.

314
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:20,920
For all the states in s,
what we do is we say--

315
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:23,800
we're going to make a new
copy of our value function,

316
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:26,680
and we say Vk of s
is equal to R of s

317
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:29,530
plus gamma sum over s
prime probability of going

318
00:13:29,530 --> 00:13:32,470
to s prime given
s times the value

319
00:13:32,470 --> 00:13:36,340
that we already have for
k minus 1 of s prime.

320
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:38,620
And we just do this over
and over and over again

321
00:13:38,620 --> 00:13:42,750
until our value
function stops changing,

322
00:13:42,750 --> 00:13:45,220
and we'll talk soon about
whether it will stop changing.

323
00:13:45,220 --> 00:13:47,820
The nice thing about
this is that it's only

324
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:50,460
s squared for each
iteration, so this

325
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:55,770
would be an iteration,
instead of a matrix inverse.

326
00:14:00,210 --> 00:14:00,850
All right.

327
00:14:00,850 --> 00:14:03,340
So this is how you could
compute the value of an MRP.

328
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:06,090
Now we're going to see how
we could do that for an MDP.

329
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:09,210
So a Markov decision process is
very similar to a Markov reward

330
00:14:09,210 --> 00:14:11,560
process, but now we
get to add in actions.

331
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:14,310
So now we're actually going to
be starting to make decisions.

332
00:14:14,310 --> 00:14:17,490
And the idea now is that
the dynamics transition

333
00:14:17,490 --> 00:14:20,528
model will probably depend
on the action you take.

334
00:14:20,528 --> 00:14:22,570
So you're going to get to
different distributions

335
00:14:22,570 --> 00:14:24,200
of next states.

336
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:26,620
And so it could be
something like you think of,

337
00:14:26,620 --> 00:14:28,670
depending on the ad
you show a customer,

338
00:14:28,670 --> 00:14:30,130
they might do different things.

339
00:14:30,130 --> 00:14:31,978
Depending on the
controls of your robot,

340
00:14:31,978 --> 00:14:34,520
it's going to move or manipulate
its hand in a different way.

341
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:35,978
Generally, these
dynamics are going

342
00:14:35,978 --> 00:14:38,530
to be a function of the
action, and we are going to,

343
00:14:38,530 --> 00:14:41,290
for right now,
assume the reward is

344
00:14:41,290 --> 00:14:44,230
a function of the state
and the action you take.

345
00:14:44,230 --> 00:14:46,960
So you often say that an
MDP is defined by a tuple--

346
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:51,220
S, A, dynamics model,
reward model, and gamma.

347
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:53,540
So we could think
of that for here.

348
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:55,430
So now we have our
same little Mars rover,

349
00:14:55,430 --> 00:14:58,580
but now we actually have two
different dynamics models,

350
00:14:58,580 --> 00:15:02,350
one for if we take a1
and one if we take a2.

351
00:15:02,350 --> 00:15:03,530
This is just an example.

352
00:15:03,530 --> 00:15:05,470
In these cases, these
are deterministic.

353
00:15:05,470 --> 00:15:07,670
In general, we can have
them be stochastic.

354
00:15:07,670 --> 00:15:10,250
And we would also need to
specify what the reward is,

355
00:15:10,250 --> 00:15:13,540
so maybe we have 0 reward
in all of these states

356
00:15:13,540 --> 00:15:16,420
and plus 1 here and
plus 10 at the end.

357
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:17,960
And this would just define.

358
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:20,690
So once you've defined the
state space, the action space,

359
00:15:20,690 --> 00:15:23,390
the reward function, the
dynamics model, and the gamma,

360
00:15:23,390 --> 00:15:24,675
then you've defined your MDP.

361
00:15:27,990 --> 00:15:28,490
All right.

362
00:15:28,490 --> 00:15:31,615
So now we actually get to start
to think about policies, which

363
00:15:31,615 --> 00:15:34,240
is what we'll be talking about
throughout the course, which is,

364
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,960
how do we make decisions
depending on the state we're in?

365
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:42,590
And the policy is going to
specify the action to take,

366
00:15:42,590 --> 00:15:44,860
which can be deterministic
or stochastic,

367
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:49,400
and often we're going to think
of it as being stochastic.

368
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,890
And we'll talk about the
properties of stochastic

369
00:15:51,890 --> 00:15:53,510
versus deterministic
ones and why

370
00:15:53,510 --> 00:15:56,610
you might want one or the
other quite a bit in the class,

371
00:15:56,610 --> 00:15:58,040
but we can generally
do everything

372
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:00,990
we're doing in each case.

373
00:16:00,990 --> 00:16:01,510
All right.

374
00:16:01,510 --> 00:16:06,850
So an MDP plus a policy is
just a Markov reward process.

375
00:16:06,850 --> 00:16:07,700
Why is that?

376
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:10,910
Because once you specify
how you're going to act,

377
00:16:10,910 --> 00:16:12,910
you've removed the
policy part, and so

378
00:16:12,910 --> 00:16:15,578
if you want to know how
good that policy is--

379
00:16:15,578 --> 00:16:17,870
so let's say someone says--
again, your boss says, hey,

380
00:16:17,870 --> 00:16:20,240
how good is this thing at
advertising to customers,

381
00:16:20,240 --> 00:16:21,250
for example?

382
00:16:21,250 --> 00:16:24,580
Then once you've decided
what the policy is,

383
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:26,320
we can think of
the reward as just

384
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,960
being a weighted sum over
the probability that's

385
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:31,900
taking that action in that state
times the reward for that state

386
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:34,780
in action and then your
dynamics model, which

387
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:36,640
is a little more subtle,
which is now you're

388
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:40,390
taking a weighted sum over
all of the transition dynamics

389
00:16:40,390 --> 00:16:43,737
according to the action you take
weighted by the probability you

390
00:16:43,737 --> 00:16:44,445
take that action.

391
00:16:47,670 --> 00:16:51,390
So it just defines
a Markov process

392
00:16:51,390 --> 00:16:54,960
because now you just have this
transformed dynamics model where

393
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:58,300
you've merged in the policy.

394
00:16:58,300 --> 00:16:59,740
So why is this helpful?

395
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:00,870
And this is something
that you may or may not

396
00:17:00,870 --> 00:17:02,120
have seen in previous classes.

397
00:17:02,120 --> 00:17:03,450
One of the reasons
why this is helpful

398
00:17:03,450 --> 00:17:04,908
is because now we
can just say, oh,

399
00:17:04,908 --> 00:17:07,530
any techniques we have for
Markov reward processes we could

400
00:17:07,530 --> 00:17:11,160
also apply to evaluating the
value of a particular policy

401
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:14,140
in a Markov decision
process because we've just

402
00:17:14,140 --> 00:17:17,460
reduced an MDP and policy
evaluation back to an MRP.

403
00:17:22,010 --> 00:17:26,430
All right, so if we think
about doing policy evaluation

404
00:17:26,430 --> 00:17:32,100
with an MDP, we can just
plug in the actual policy

405
00:17:32,100 --> 00:17:34,980
that we would be using.

406
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:39,350
So what we have in this case
is that instead of-- now we

407
00:17:39,350 --> 00:17:42,053
actually get to make decisions,
and so then we get to say,

408
00:17:42,053 --> 00:17:43,470
what is the
probability of picking

409
00:17:43,470 --> 00:17:46,632
the action in this state
times the expected discounted

410
00:17:46,632 --> 00:17:47,840
sum of rewards at that point?

411
00:17:50,390 --> 00:17:52,640
So this looks very
similar to an MRP,

412
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:54,860
except for we're saying,
based on the probability

413
00:17:54,860 --> 00:17:57,890
for each action, what
would we get next?

414
00:17:57,890 --> 00:18:03,250
And we call this a Bellman
backup for a particular policy

415
00:18:03,250 --> 00:18:06,520
because this is going to specify
what is our expected discounted

416
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:10,450
sum of future rewards if
we start in this state

417
00:18:10,450 --> 00:18:12,800
and follow the policy?

418
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:14,990
And just notice that if
the policy is actually

419
00:18:14,990 --> 00:18:18,140
deterministic, we can
reduce it back to a case

420
00:18:18,140 --> 00:18:22,470
where we've sort of
averaged over these rewards.

421
00:18:22,470 --> 00:18:26,930
So remember, this
was just going to be

422
00:18:26,930 --> 00:18:28,927
if you have a
particular action, then

423
00:18:28,927 --> 00:18:31,010
you're just going to index
into what the reward is

424
00:18:31,010 --> 00:18:32,250
for that particular action.

425
00:18:32,250 --> 00:18:35,500
So we can see that here.

426
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,120
And just raise your hand
if you've seen this before,

427
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:39,770
if you've seen the [INAUDIBLE].

428
00:18:39,770 --> 00:18:43,790
OK, good, so probably
at least 2/3 of people.

429
00:18:43,790 --> 00:18:44,350
All right.

430
00:18:47,030 --> 00:18:47,530
OK.

431
00:18:47,530 --> 00:18:50,260
So if you want to check your
answers, if some of this

432
00:18:50,260 --> 00:18:52,270
is new for you,
then one thing to do

433
00:18:52,270 --> 00:18:56,370
is to try to check that you can
do this sort of value iteration

434
00:18:56,370 --> 00:18:59,135
or this policy evaluation
for the Mars rover example.

435
00:18:59,135 --> 00:19:01,760
We won't go through it in class,
but you can check the answers.

436
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:03,677
I'll release them at the
end of the slide just

437
00:19:03,677 --> 00:19:06,610
to check that you know
how to apply this.

438
00:19:06,610 --> 00:19:07,335
All right.

439
00:19:07,335 --> 00:19:08,710
So of course,
shortly we're going

440
00:19:08,710 --> 00:19:10,335
to be interested in
not just evaluating

441
00:19:10,335 --> 00:19:13,240
the value of a single policy
but finding an optimal policy.

442
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:16,000
So one question is, how
many policies are there?

443
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:21,313
And is the optimal
policy value unique?

444
00:19:21,313 --> 00:19:22,480
So we'll just take a second.

445
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:24,530
You can go to the polls
and enter in your answer.

446
00:19:35,420 --> 00:19:36,030
OK, great.

447
00:19:36,030 --> 00:19:37,740
So it looks like
most people got--

448
00:19:37,740 --> 00:19:39,740
the vast majority of
people got the right answer

449
00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:42,420
for the first one, which
is it's 2 to the 7.

450
00:19:42,420 --> 00:19:44,600
In general, the number
of policies we have

451
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:50,270
is going to be A to the S
because for every single state

452
00:19:50,270 --> 00:19:52,640
we could choose
any of the actions.

453
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:55,800
And also, most people got the
next one right, which is great,

454
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:58,880
which is the optimal policy,
the one with the highest value,

455
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:01,280
is not always unique.

456
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,770
It can be unique-- it
depends on the problem--

457
00:20:03,770 --> 00:20:06,110
but it's not going
to be unique whenever

458
00:20:06,110 --> 00:20:09,613
more than one action has
the same identical value,

459
00:20:09,613 --> 00:20:10,530
so when you have ties.

460
00:20:10,530 --> 00:20:13,020
Yeah?

461
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:16,360
How do we generally deal
with invalid actions?

462
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:21,450
Because for example, if we're
in S1 and we choose left,

463
00:20:21,450 --> 00:20:24,690
I would imagine-- to me,
that's an invalid action.

464
00:20:24,690 --> 00:20:26,940
I'm not sure how we
really deal with that.

465
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:29,740
Yeah, so the question was
if we have invalid actions.

466
00:20:29,740 --> 00:20:32,790
So in general, you can have
a different action space

467
00:20:32,790 --> 00:20:34,440
be possible in every state.

468
00:20:34,440 --> 00:20:36,990
That's also very common
in recommendation engines

469
00:20:36,990 --> 00:20:39,240
that you'd only-- it's
only a subset of articles

470
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,550
you might show to some
people based on their state.

471
00:20:41,550 --> 00:20:43,050
In this particular
example, we're

472
00:20:43,050 --> 00:20:45,550
going to assume that it's
not actually go left.

473
00:20:45,550 --> 00:20:46,390
It's try left.

474
00:20:46,390 --> 00:20:47,580
And so if you try to
go left and there's

475
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:48,490
nothing in the
rest of the world,

476
00:20:48,490 --> 00:20:50,430
you just fail, and you
stay in the same place.

477
00:20:50,430 --> 00:20:52,780
But in general, most of
the time in the class,

478
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:53,910
we're going to assume
the action space is

479
00:20:53,910 --> 00:20:55,750
the same for all states,
but in some cases,

480
00:20:55,750 --> 00:20:56,677
it might be different.

481
00:20:56,677 --> 00:20:57,260
Good question.

482
00:21:01,490 --> 00:21:01,990
OK.

483
00:21:01,990 --> 00:21:03,960
So in MDP control,
we're going to want

484
00:21:03,960 --> 00:21:06,060
to not just have the policy--

485
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:08,160
evaluate a particular
policy, but we're going

486
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:09,890
to compute the optimal policy.

487
00:21:09,890 --> 00:21:12,540
So we want to take the arg max
over the policy space, which

488
00:21:12,540 --> 00:21:16,080
in general is that
A to the S space,

489
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:20,580
and there is going to exist a
unique optimal value function.

490
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:24,030
And the optimal policy
inside of a tabular MDP

491
00:21:24,030 --> 00:21:28,030
in an infinite horizon is
unique and deterministic.

492
00:21:28,030 --> 00:21:32,898
So those are two properties that
are good to be familiar with.

493
00:21:32,898 --> 00:21:34,440
So now we're going
to think about how

494
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:38,080
do we actually compute this and
what its other properties are.

495
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:40,590
So one is that it's stationary.

496
