you is how to act forever

983
00:47:17,910 --> 00:47:19,020
using that policy.

984
00:47:19,020 --> 00:47:20,920
And when you
compute the Q-value,

985
00:47:20,920 --> 00:47:22,978
it says, how much
reward do you get if you

986
00:47:22,978 --> 00:47:24,270
take this action in this state?

987
00:47:24,270 --> 00:47:26,880
And then follow that
other policy forever.

988
00:47:26,880 --> 00:47:30,720
So again, remember, today we're
in the infinite horizon case

989
00:47:30,720 --> 00:47:34,660
unless I specify otherwise.

990
00:47:34,660 --> 00:47:37,290
But along the way, a lot of
those actions and the decisions

991
00:47:37,290 --> 00:47:38,530
we make may not be very good.

992
00:47:38,530 --> 00:47:40,517
So your early policies
might be pretty bad.

993
00:47:40,517 --> 00:47:42,100
We know we're
monotonically improving,

994
00:47:42,100 --> 00:47:44,160
but the early
policies might be bad.

995
00:47:44,160 --> 00:47:46,330
Value iteration is different.

996
00:47:46,330 --> 00:47:49,032
The idea is that
at every iteration,

997
00:47:49,032 --> 00:47:51,240
we're going to maintain the
optimal value of starting

998
00:47:51,240 --> 00:47:53,550
in a state but as if
we only get to make

999
00:47:53,550 --> 00:47:55,950
a finite number of decisions.

1000
00:47:55,950 --> 00:47:57,970
So remember, in
policy iteration,

1001
00:47:57,970 --> 00:48:01,390
we always have a policy, and we
have the value of acting in it

1002
00:48:01,390 --> 00:48:02,030
forever.

1003
00:48:02,030 --> 00:48:03,410
It just might not be very good.

1004
00:48:03,410 --> 00:48:06,970
Value iteration is, what
is the optimal thing for me

1005
00:48:06,970 --> 00:48:10,132
to do if I can just make one
decision, I can take one step?

1006
00:48:10,132 --> 00:48:12,340
OK, I'm going to figure out
what the optimal thing is

1007
00:48:12,340 --> 00:48:13,598
to do for one step.

1008
00:48:13,598 --> 00:48:15,640
Now I'm going to imagine
I get to take two steps,

1009
00:48:15,640 --> 00:48:18,605
and I'm going to build on what
I know I can do for one step.

1010
00:48:18,605 --> 00:48:20,230
And so now I'll build
the optimal thing

1011
00:48:20,230 --> 00:48:21,940
to do for two steps.

1012
00:48:21,940 --> 00:48:23,860
So the interesting thing
with value iteration

1013
00:48:23,860 --> 00:48:27,850
is you always have an optimal
value but for the wrong horizon.

1014
00:48:27,850 --> 00:48:32,510
So one has a value for
the infinite horizon.

1015
00:48:32,510 --> 00:48:33,770
It might be a bad policy.

1016
00:48:33,770 --> 00:48:36,610
The other one has the
optimal value and thing

1017
00:48:36,610 --> 00:48:40,010
to do but for the wrong horizon.

1018
00:48:40,010 --> 00:48:41,530
And the idea in
value iteration is

1019
00:48:41,530 --> 00:48:44,290
you just keep going to longer
and longer and longer episodes,

1020
00:48:44,290 --> 00:48:47,930
thinking of getting to do H
plus 1 steps or H plus 2 steps,

1021
00:48:47,930 --> 00:48:49,930
and then you build upon
your previous solutions

1022
00:48:49,930 --> 00:48:51,760
using dynamic programming.

1023
00:48:51,760 --> 00:48:53,920
So let's see how to do that.

1024
00:48:53,920 --> 00:48:57,090
OK, so this is where we get
into the Bellman equation.

1025
00:48:57,090 --> 00:49:00,070
This is the seminal
work of Richard Bellman.

1026
00:49:00,070 --> 00:49:02,300
And the idea here
is, as we've said,

1027
00:49:02,300 --> 00:49:06,550
is that for a particular policy,
we satisfy the Bellman equation,

1028
00:49:06,550 --> 00:49:10,960
and we can turn that
into an algorithm.

1029
00:49:10,960 --> 00:49:13,600
So in particular, there's a
thing called the Bellman backup

1030
00:49:13,600 --> 00:49:16,330
operator, and what it
says is if you give me

1031
00:49:16,330 --> 00:49:18,625
a value function, which
right now we can think

1032
00:49:18,625 --> 00:49:19,750
of as just being a vector--

1033
00:49:19,750 --> 00:49:22,180
later we'll get into
function approximation--

1034
00:49:22,180 --> 00:49:24,340
and we do a Bellman
backup, essentially, it's

1035
00:49:24,340 --> 00:49:26,543
like saying, I had
a value function,

1036
00:49:26,543 --> 00:49:28,210
and I want to think
about, what should I

1037
00:49:28,210 --> 00:49:30,430
do if I get to do the
best thing that maximizes

1038
00:49:30,430 --> 00:49:34,060
my immediate reward plus my
expected future reward given

1039
00:49:34,060 --> 00:49:35,800
that value function?

1040
00:49:35,800 --> 00:49:38,080
So it says, I'm
going to figure out,

1041
00:49:38,080 --> 00:49:40,570
if I take a max over
all the actions, what's

1042
00:49:40,570 --> 00:49:42,230
the reward of that
state in the action

1043
00:49:42,230 --> 00:49:45,130
plus the discounted sum
of rewards using the value

1044
00:49:45,130 --> 00:49:48,120
function you've given me?

1045
00:49:48,120 --> 00:49:51,540
And what that does is
it yields a new vector

1046
00:49:51,540 --> 00:49:54,570
of values over all your states.

1047
00:49:54,570 --> 00:49:57,968
So this is being done
per state, and this

1048
00:49:57,968 --> 00:49:59,260
is called the Bellman operator.

1049
00:49:59,260 --> 00:50:00,302
It comes up all the time.

1050
00:50:03,130 --> 00:50:04,540
All right.

1051
00:50:04,540 --> 00:50:06,670
So how do we do value iteration?

1052
00:50:06,670 --> 00:50:09,820
Well, we're just going
to do this recursively,

1053
00:50:09,820 --> 00:50:13,690
so we're just going to loop
until we hit convergence.

1054
00:50:13,690 --> 00:50:18,320
Just as a refresher, this
is the L-infinity norm,

1055
00:50:18,320 --> 00:50:20,290
and what that
means is that it is

1056
00:50:20,290 --> 00:50:25,560
equal to the max over S V of s.

1057
00:50:25,560 --> 00:50:34,430
Maybe I'll write it out more
carefully, equal to max over

1058
00:50:34,430 --> 00:50:40,070
S Vk plus 1 of s minus Vk of s.

1059
00:50:40,070 --> 00:50:43,440
What that just means is that
if you have two vectors,

1060
00:50:43,440 --> 00:50:45,560
you look for every
single entry, and you

1061
00:50:45,560 --> 00:50:49,420
find the entry in which those
two vectors are most different.

1062
00:50:49,420 --> 00:50:51,003
And that's the
L-infinity norm just as

1063
00:50:51,003 --> 00:50:53,253
a refresher for some of you
who might not have seen it

1064
00:50:53,253 --> 00:50:54,490
or not seen it recently.

1065
00:50:54,490 --> 00:50:58,368
So what value iteration does is
we're just going to have a loop.

1066
00:50:58,368 --> 00:51:00,910
It's going to look very similar
to what we saw for the Markov

1067
00:51:00,910 --> 00:51:02,290
reward process.

1068
00:51:02,290 --> 00:51:04,210
We're going to initialize
our value function,

1069
00:51:04,210 --> 00:51:05,710
and then for each
state, we're just

1070
00:51:05,710 --> 00:51:08,020
