1465
01:09:03,569 --> 01:09:08,495
So it would compute a series
of policies, one for each step.

1466
01:09:13,286 --> 01:09:15,250
One other thing I
want to mention here--

1467
01:09:15,250 --> 01:09:18,404
and we'll talk about
this on the homework too.

1468
01:09:18,404 --> 01:09:20,279
One of the other thing
I want to mention here

1469
01:09:20,279 --> 01:09:22,740
is that you can
also just simulate

1470
01:09:22,740 --> 01:09:27,127
the value of a
particular policy.

1471
01:09:27,127 --> 01:09:28,710
So this is also
really popular once we

1472
01:09:28,710 --> 01:09:31,020
start to get into
really big state spaces.

1473
01:09:31,020 --> 01:09:33,670
So if we think of the fact that
in a lot of these algorithms

1474
01:09:33,670 --> 01:09:35,979
we're doing some sort
of policy evaluation,

1475
01:09:35,979 --> 01:09:38,521
one thing you could do is
you just take your policy.

1476
01:09:38,521 --> 01:09:40,229
And you know what your
dynamics model is,

1477
01:09:40,229 --> 01:09:41,604
and you know what
your reward is.

1478
01:09:41,604 --> 01:09:42,990
And you just roll it out.

1479
01:09:42,990 --> 01:09:44,970
So if you're in a
state, you simulate

1480
01:09:44,970 --> 01:09:46,390
what the next state might be.

1481
01:09:46,390 --> 01:09:48,600
Then you get a reward.

1482
01:09:48,600 --> 01:09:51,330
So you can just generate a
really large number of episodes

1483
01:09:51,330 --> 01:09:53,189
and then just average them.

1484
01:09:53,189 --> 01:09:55,812
So I'm just like, oh,
how good is this policy?

1485
01:09:55,812 --> 01:09:58,020
If your boss asks you, you
just run it on 100 people.

1486
01:09:58,020 --> 01:10:00,870
You average their rewards,
and then you're done.

1487
01:10:00,870 --> 01:10:03,270
And this is something that
becomes really popular when

1488
01:10:03,270 --> 01:10:05,640
it starts to be, say,
hard to write down

1489
01:10:05,640 --> 01:10:07,290
what that dynamics
model is explicitly

1490
01:10:07,290 --> 01:10:09,490
or do that sum over s prime.

1491
01:10:09,490 --> 01:10:11,850
But it's really easy to sample.

1492
01:10:11,850 --> 01:10:15,700
And I'll note for that you can
use concentration inequalities,

1493
01:10:15,700 --> 01:10:19,060
like Hoeffding inequality and
Bernstein, for those of you

1494
01:10:19,060 --> 01:10:22,030
familiar with them,
to bound how much data

1495
01:10:22,030 --> 01:10:26,830
do you need for this estimate
to be close to the true one.

1496
01:10:26,830 --> 01:10:29,270
And the great thing is
that it's not that many,

1497
01:10:29,270 --> 01:10:32,170
so if you have an enormous
state space, like, I don't know,

1498
01:10:32,170 --> 01:10:33,800
your Amazon or
something like that,

1499
01:10:33,800 --> 01:10:35,980
or you've got patient data,
and it's incredibly high

1500
01:10:35,980 --> 01:10:39,490
dimensional, you don't have to
do that huge sum over S prime.

1501
01:10:39,490 --> 01:10:43,000
You can just sample, and
your accuracy generally

1502
01:10:43,000 --> 01:10:44,980
improves by 1 over
square root n, the number

1503
01:10:44,980 --> 01:10:46,210
of samples you're doing.

1504
01:10:46,210 --> 01:10:47,710
And the nice thing
is that this also

1505
01:10:47,710 --> 01:10:50,567
requires no assumption
about the Markov structure.

1506
01:10:50,567 --> 01:10:52,900
So you might have a partially
observable scenario, which

1507
01:10:52,900 --> 01:10:54,840
also comes up a lot in
things like healthcare,

1508
01:10:54,840 --> 01:10:56,590
and then you can just
roll out your policy

1509
01:10:56,590 --> 01:10:58,585
and just see how well it works.

1510
01:10:58,585 --> 01:10:59,960
Well, in healthcare,
you probably

1511
01:10:59,960 --> 01:11:01,752
wouldn't just randomly
roll out any policy,

1512
01:11:01,752 --> 01:11:03,590
but pricing might be.

1513
01:11:03,590 --> 01:11:04,400
Yeah?

1514
01:11:04,400 --> 01:11:06,240
Remind me your name.

1515
01:11:06,240 --> 01:11:09,705
[INAUDIBLE]

1516
01:11:16,078 --> 01:11:16,620
That's right.

1517
01:11:16,620 --> 01:11:19,822
So here this is just the policy
evaluation stage, exactly,

1518
01:11:19,822 --> 01:11:22,280
and you could either do it to
compute the value of a policy

1519
01:11:22,280 --> 01:11:25,520
or, as you just suggested,
do it for the Q-value.

1520
01:11:25,520 --> 01:11:28,560
So you start off in a state for
each of the different actions.

1521
01:11:28,560 --> 01:11:30,920
Then you roll out the
policy until the end.

1522
01:11:30,920 --> 01:11:33,410
And this is just a really
popular other technique,

1523
01:11:33,410 --> 01:11:35,130
and it'll come up
in other places.

1524
01:11:35,130 --> 01:11:38,730
So I wanted to start saying,
we can do that here too.

1525
01:11:38,730 --> 01:11:41,870
So you can also think
about doing all of these

1526
01:11:41,870 --> 01:11:43,350
in the case of the Mars rover.

1527
01:11:43,350 --> 01:11:45,860
And I won't go through
it now, but you

1528
01:11:45,860 --> 01:11:47,810
can use these as
examples to step

1529
01:11:47,810 --> 01:11:49,790
through these
different algorithms

1530
01:11:49,790 --> 01:11:54,470
and think about how you would
compute these type of policies.

1531
01:11:54,470 --> 01:11:56,780
All right.

1532
01:11:56,780 --> 01:12:00,720
So I will-- maybe I'll
get to the end of this,

1533
01:12:00,720 --> 01:12:02,520
but I'll leave you
with two things.

1534
01:12:02,520 --> 01:12:04,880
One is a thought
question, which is,

1535
01:12:04,880 --> 01:12:06,617
is the optimal
policy stationary?

1536
01:12:06,617 --> 01:12:08,450
What that means is
independent of time steps

1537
01:12:08,450 --> 01:12:10,400
in the finite horizon tasks.

1538
01:12:10,400 --> 01:12:14,360
And we'll explore this
issue, too, on the homework.

1539
01:12:14,360 --> 01:12:19,050
And I also just want to
refresh some terminology.

1540
01:12:19,050 --> 01:12:21,320
So in the context of
Markov decision processes

1541
01:12:21,320 --> 01:12:23,645
and reinforcement learning,
when we say, "models,"

1542
01:12:23,645 --> 01:12:25,520
what we normally mean
is a mathematical model

1543
01:12:25,520 --> 01:12:27,320
of the dynamics and reward.

1544
01:12:27,320 --> 01:12:29,300
Policy is a function
mapping from states

1545
01:12:29,300 --> 01:12:32,010
to actions that can be
deterministic or stochastic,

1546
01:12:32,010 --> 01:12:34,430
and the value function is
this expected discounted

1547
01:12:34,430 --> 01:12:38,730
sum of rewards from starting in
a state and following a policy.

1548
01:12:38,730 --> 01:12:41,230
The things that should be clear
to you is you should be able

1549
01:12:41,230 --> 01:12:42,897
to understand what a
Markov process is--

1550
01:12:42,897 --> 01:12:45,430
a Markov reward
process is an MDP--

1551
01:12:45,430 --> 01:12:48,160
the Bellman operator,
contraction, model, Q-value,

1552
01:12:48,160 --> 01:12:48,677
and policy.

1553
01:12:48,677 --> 01:12:51,010
And you should be able to
implement both value iteration

1554
01:12:51,010 --> 01:12:52,660
and policy iteration,
and you'll have practice

1555
01:12:52,660 --> 01:12:53,960
doing that on the homework.

1556
01:12:53,960 --> 01:12:55,990
You should also understand some
of the strengths and weaknesses

1557
01:12:55,990 --> 01:12:57,698
in terms of what we've
discussed in terms

1558
01:12:57,698 --> 01:12:59,530
of the computational
complexity of some

1559
01:12:59,530 --> 01:13:01,300
of these different
operations and be

1560
01:13:01,300 --> 01:13:04,180
able to prove contraction
properties about these

1561
01:13:04,180 --> 01:13:07,360
as well as understand which
of these are really leveraging

1562
01:13:07,360 --> 01:13:09,100
the Markov assumption
versus which

1563
01:13:09,100 --> 01:13:11,650
of them don't require that.

1564
01:13:11,650 --> 01:13:13,930
So next week we'll continue
to talk about this,

1565
01:13:13,930 --> 01:13:16,240
and we'll start to talk
about function approximation

1566
01:13:16,240 --> 01:13:17,980
and how we can also
learn when we don't

1567
01:13:17,980 --> 01:13:19,610
know what these models are.

1568
01:13:19,610 --> 01:13:20,440
I'll see you then.

1569
01:13:20,440 --> 01:13:21,690
Thanks

